#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The switcher, composited OVER the game by gamescope's overlay plane.

    switcher-overlay --display :1              # one-shot: draw, drive, exit
    switcher-overlay --display :1 --resident   # the per-session daemon

Donnie, 15 Aug 2026, the night the first wrapped game ran: "can we get the
switcher showing faster and not going to kodi first? or just having it over
the running game?" This is that. It draws the same window rail the Kodi
switcher and the phone show, as a gamescope external overlay INSIDE the
nested display, over the game the compositor is already drawing. No Kodi
raise, no freeze-frame capture, no restack - the "game behind the menu" is
gamescope's own surface, and because the caller froze the emulator first,
the pad cannot leak into the game while the rail is up.

Shape stolen from tools/pipd, which earned it the hard way (read its module
docstring): the overlay plane blits at 0,0 output-sized and IGNORES the X
window's own position, so the plane is a FULLSCREEN 32-bit window and the
whole deck is painted into a fullscreen CHILD window inside it.

The LOOK is the Kodi switcher's deck, recreated in PIL ("can we just copy
the xml over then? and use that one for everything" - Donnie, 15 Aug).
script-couch-switcher.xml is the source of truth: the eased deck-scrim
gradient (the real texture, stretched, so the curve cannot drift), the
POWER capsule bar, the SWITCH TO art rail, one focus grammar - THE FOCUSED
THING TURNS WHITE (capsules fill white with ink text, tiles get a white
ring and their title beneath). The XML is authored in 1080i coordinates;
everything here scales by out_h/1080. The freeze-frame backdrop the Kodi
dialog fakes with a property is free here: the frozen game itself is
already behind the plane.

Switching logic: NONE, deliberately, the same contract as the Kodi addon
and the phone. Rows come from GET /api/windows on the couch server, a pick
POSTs /api/windows/activate, and everything that happens next (a paused
game resumes, Kodi raises with the game left frozen) is decided in
server/screen.js exactly as it is for the other two remotes. The power bar
is the same story: a pick dispatches Addons.ExecuteAddon to
script.tvpoweroff over Kodi's JSON-RPC, byte-identical to what the Kodi
dialog sends, so power logic keeps its one home. And the spotify pills are
the same story again: drawn only while GET /api/spotify says a phone
session is live, clicks POST /api/spotify/<cmd> and redraw from the answer
WITHOUT closing the deck (skipping is a repeat-press activity), explicit
play/pause verbs rather than a toggle - all of it default.py's rules.

The pad is read RAW from /dev/input (struct input_event is 24 bytes; logind
ACLs the seat user onto the nodes) because evdev-the-library lives only in
couchd's venv and this tool runs on the system python for PIL. The game is
frozen, so double-reading the device steals nothing from anyone.

THE RESIDENT MODE (15 Aug 2026, evening - "takes a while to appear... have
it wait in the background?"). --resident makes the rail live WITH the game
session instead of per double-tap: game-launch starts it right after the
display bridge lands, it builds its X window immediately but UNMAPPED
(gamescope skips unmapped planes entirely - steamcompmgr only paints mapped
commits - so a hidden rail costs the compositor nothing per frame), and it
pre-fetches rows/thumbs and pre-composes the sheet down to the BGRA bytes
PutImage wants, refreshing them lazily while hidden. A double-tap is then
map-plus-draw: couchd pokes the control socket (pipd's daemon+socket shape,
/tmp/switcher-overlayd.sock, JSON lines) and the deck is on screen in tens
of milliseconds instead of a cold python+PIL+HTTP spawn. Every show logs
`signal-to-mapped` so the claim is measured, not felt.

The contract does not move:
  * /tmp/switcher-overlay means MAPPED AND OWNING THE PAD, not "process
    alive" - the resident writes it on map and clears it on unmap, so
    couchd's effect check and want_pad_owner read exactly what they always
    read, and a hidden resident leaves the world byte-identical to no rail
    at all.
  * Kodi's joystick toggles on map/unmap by the same rules as the one-shot
    exits (a paused pick's resume owns its own routing; see finish_steps).
  * The frame-wait runs only when a show actually maps, never at daemon
    start.
  * The singleton holds across both modes: a mapped rail (either mode)
    stands a second one down, and a newer resident retires an older one.
The one-shot mode stays byte-for-byte in behaviour (exit codes 0/1/2/3
below) and is couchd's fallback when no resident answers; a one-shot that
finds a live resident hands the show over and stands down.

Exit codes, for the caller that wires this to a gesture (--resident exits
0 on a clean shutdown and 2 broken; these are the ONE-SHOT's):
  0  a row was activated (the server owns what that meant)
  1  cancelled with B - and unless --no-resume-on-cancel, the paused row
     was re-activated first, which is the server's own "resume" path
  2  broken (no display, no rows, no pad...)
  3  superseded: the suspended flag cleared underneath us (the PS button's
     own resume, say) - the rail closed itself and did nothing

Test seams (unset in normal operation):
  SWITCHER_OVERLAY_ROWS    path to a JSON file used instead of the HTTP GET
  SWITCHER_OVERLAY_SPOTIFY path to a JSON file instead of GET /api/spotify
  SWITCHER_OVERLAY_NO_PAD  do not open input devices (rig runs headless)
  SWITCHER_OVERLAY_ONESHOT render once, screenshot-able, exit 0 on SIGTERM
  SWITCHER_OVERLAY_SOCK    control-socket path override (tests bind a fake)
  SWITCHER_OVERLAY_SUSPENDED / SWITCHER_OVERLAY_BRIDGE
                           path overrides for the two /tmp flags, so the
                           headless rig never touches couchd's watched names
"""
